  <h2>Where to find a classic car’s VIN</h2>
  <p>Location changed by maker and year. Common spots are the driver-side door hinge pillar (for example the 1967 Camaro), the top of the dash visible through the windshield (most cars from 1968 on), and the inner fender apron, dash or door on 1960s Mustangs. Many cars also carry a partial VIN stamped on the engine block and transmission. Matching those stampings to the VIN plate is how “numbers-matching” is confirmed.</p>

  <h2>What a classic VIN can’t tell you</h2>
  <p>A pre-1981 serial won’t show accidents, title brands, past owners, color or most options. Those live on the trim or cowl tag, fender tag, build sheet or Marti report, and in title records. Older cars also aren’t covered by NHTSA’s online recall lookup. If you’re buying, also run a title check through an <a href="https://vehiclehistory.bja.ojp.gov/nmvtis_vehiclehistory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">approved NMVTIS provider</a> and have the car inspected by someone who knows the model.</p>

  <h2>Spotting a re-stamped or swapped VIN</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rivets that don’t match the factory type for that plant and year (rosette-style rivets were common on GM dash plates).</li>
    <li>A VIN whose plant, year or sequence doesn’t fit the model. For example, a 1970 Charger not built in St. Louis, or a Camaro sequence below that year’s starting number.</li>
    <li>Engine or transmission partial VINs that don’t match the plate, or show signs of grinding and re-stamping.</li>
    <li>A paper trail (title, build sheet, Protect-O-Plate) that disagrees with the plate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-sm">Car Alpha flags some of these automatically when the code tables allow it. It can’t replace a hands-on inspection.</p>
